          "text": "{\"schema_version\":\"ai-evaluator-v2/1.1\",\"case_id\":\"3-4\",\"candidate_id\":\"candidate_3\",\"action_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The graph captures sending the initial questionnaire and sending reminders, but it incorrectly repeats the questionnaire-sending action on each retry cycle, which is unsupported by the source.\"},\"flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"preserved\",\"summary\":\"Required matched-action precedence is broadly preserved: the questionnaire is sent before reminders, and the process can continue until receipt. The main problem is not ordering between matched source actions but the added repeated questionnaire action.\"},\"control_flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The recurrence is modeled incorrectly: after a reminder, the loop returns to sending the questionnaire again instead of repeating the reminder cycle until the completed questionnaire is received.\"},\"main_error_type\":\"wrong_loop_or_recurrence\",\"explanation\":\"The source process sends the questionnaire once, then if no response is received after successive five-day intervals, sends repeated reminders until the completed questionnaire arrives. The candidate preserves the overall reminder-until-response idea and can reasonably leave the waiting periods implicit, but its retry path goes from the reminder back to 'send questionnaire to claimant.' That makes the repeated behavior wrong: each retry cycle resends the questionnaire rather than only continuing the reminder cycle. This is a meaningful semantic defect because it changes what action recurs, though the core process intent remains partly recognizable.\",\"overall_score\":0.69,\"severity\":\"moderate\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"ambiguity_flag\":false,\"ambiguity_explanation\":\"\",\"requires_human_review\":false,\"review_reason\":\"\"}"
        }
